[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **None.** Every input class enumerated below produced byte-identical stdout,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-identical stderr (empty in all cases) and an identical exit status (0).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m That is an unusual result, so the sections that follow record what was actually
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exercised, and how the test harness itself was validated — an all-green suite
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is only meaningful if it can fail.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Harness validation (mutation testing)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m To prove the comparison is real rather than vacuous, `src/main.rs` was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m temporarily mutated three times and the suite re-run. The original file was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m restored and byte-compared (`cmp`) afterwards.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Mutation | Result |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `to_ne_bytes()` → `to_be_bytes()` (byte order) | suite FAILED, reported as `stdout mismatch` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `return 0` → `std::process::exit(1)` | suite FAILED, reported as `exit status mismatch` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | scanf matching-failure path returns `Some(0.5)` instead of `None` | suite FAILED (5 tests) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So a wrong value, a wrong exit status, and wrong failure semantics are all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m caught.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours that were specifically checked because they are easy to get wrong
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places a translation would most plausibly diverge. Each was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m confirmed identical rather than assumed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`scanf` failure leaves `x` untouched.** On matching failure (`abc`, `.`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `+`, `0x`, `infin`) and on input failure (empty stdin, whitespace-only,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    closed fd 0) nothing is stored, so the program prints `00000000` and still
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    exits 0. A translation that defaulted to NaN, or that exited non-zero on a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    parse error, would diverge here. Covered by `empty_input_leaves_x_at_zero`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and `matching_failure_paths`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **Whitespace is skipped across newlines.** `%f` skips leading
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    ` \t\n\v\f\r`, so `"\n\n\n\t\v\f\r  -2.5"` parses. Only the first token is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    read: `1.5 2.5` and `1.5\n2.5` both yield `1.5`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `whitespace_skipping_and_early_stop`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **Incomplete exponents are pushed back, not consumed.** `1e`, `1e+`, `1.5e`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `0x1p`, `0x1p-` all parse as the significand alone and succeed; they are not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    matching failures. Covered by `incomplete_exponent_is_pushed_back`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **`0x` with no significand is a matching failure, not the value 0.** Once
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the scanner commits to the hex prefix it requires a hex digit or a radix
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    point, so `0x`, `0X`, `0xg`, `0xp1` store nothing and print `00000000` —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    which happens to look like a successful parse of `0`, but is reached by a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    different path. Covered by `matching_failure_paths` and `hex_float_forms`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. **`inf` vs `infinity` prefix commitment.** `inf` succeeds; `infi`, `infin`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `infini`, `infinit` are matching failures; `infinityx` succeeds and stops
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    before `x`. Covered by `infinity_and_prefixes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6. **`nan` with an n-char-sequence payload.** `nan(1)`, `nan(abc_123)`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `nan(abc` (unterminated) and `-nan(0x7)` all yield the same default quiet
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    NaN pattern as bare `nan`; the payload never reaches the stored value. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    NaN sign bit is preserved for `-nan`. Covered by `nan_forms`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 7. **C99 hex floats are accepted by glibc's `%f`.** `0x1p3`, `0x1.8p1`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `0x.8p1`, `0x1.8` (no exponent), and 40-hex-digit significands with `p-200`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    all round identically. Covered by `hex_float_forms`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 8. **Correct rounding, including exact ties.** Exact midpoints between adjacent
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    binary32 values were generated with big-decimal arithmetic (no floating
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    point) for mantissa/exponent pairs spanning subnormals through the top
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    binade, each tested bare (ties-to-even), with a `1` appended, with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `0000000000000000001` appended, and negated. Also `16777215/16/17/18`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `16777216.5`, `16777218.5`, and `1.000000059604644775390625`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `exact_ties_across_the_exponent_range` and `rounding_boundaries`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 9. **Overflow / underflow edges.** `FLT_MAX` written out in full (39 digits),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the exact overflow threshold `340282356779733661637539395458142568448` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the value one below it, smallest normal, largest subnormal, smallest
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    subnormal, exactly half the smallest subnormal (ties to zero) and one ulp of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    decimal either side of it. `0x1p128`, `0x1p-150`, `0x1.0000001p-150`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Covered by `magnitude_extremes` and `hex_float_forms`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 10. **Signed zero.** `-0`, `-0.0`, `-0e5`, `-0x0p0` produce `00000080` (sign bit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     set), not `00000000`. Covered by `single_value_decimal`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     `decimal_exponent_forms`, `hex_float_forms`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 11. **Exponents that overflow every integer width.** `1e2147483647`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     `1e-2147483648`, `1e9223372036854775807`, `1e-9223372036854775808`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     `1e+9999999999999999999999`, a 70-digit exponent, and `0e999999999999999999`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     (zero significand with an absurd exponent, which must stay zero rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     becoming inf). Covered by `absurd_exponents`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 12. **Very long significands.** 40 to 100 000 digits, including lengths
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     straddling the 800-digit truncation point in the Rust decimal path
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     (`800`, `801`, `900`), and 5000-hex-digit significands. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     `very_long_significands`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 13. **Bytes that are not valid UTF-8, and embedded NULs.** The C program is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     byte-oriented; the Rust one reads raw bytes and must not choke. `\xff`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     `\x80\x81`, `\xc3\x28`, `1.5\xff`, `\x00`, `\x001.5`, a UTF-8 BOM before
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     the number, and U+2009 (a Unicode space that is *not* C whitespace, so it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     is a matching failure). Covered by `non_utf8_and_nul_bytes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 14. **Environmental edge cases** (checked manually, outside the test file, as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     they need shell redirection): stdin closed (`0<&-`), stdin `/dev/null`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     stdout closed (`>&-`), stdout `/dev/full` (write always fails), and extra
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     `argv` entries. All four produced identical exit status and stderr; neither
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     program reports a write failure or inspects `argv`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Notes on the translation's approach
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust program reimplements the `%f` scanner and `strtof` rather than calling
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m libc. That is a correctness risk on paper, and it is why the tie-breaking,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m truncation-boundary and absurd-exponent classes above were tested so heavily
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rather than sampled. Two implementation details are worth flagging for a future
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reader:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - The 800-digit truncation with a sticky digit in `decimal_to_f32` is only safe
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   because 800 digits is far past the longest exact halfway case for binary32.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Tests at 800/801/900/2000/5000 digits confirm it empirically.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `parse_hex_float` does its own shift/round arithmetic in `u128`. The suite was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   run in the debug profile as well as release, so any arithmetic overflow on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   these paths would have panicked (debug overflow checks) and been reported as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   an exit-status mismatch. It did not.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Residual risk
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verification is empirical, against glibc on this Linux/x86-64 host. Two things
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are therefore *not* proven:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `scanf`'s treatment of `nan(payload)`, and the exact NaN bit pattern, are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   implementation-defined. On a libc that forwards the payload, or a platform
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   with a different default NaN encoding, the C output would change and the Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   program — which hardcodes `0x7fc00000` — would not follow.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - The C program uses the native byte order via `memcpy`; the Rust program uses
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `to_ne_bytes()`. These agree on any single target, including a big-endian one.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Reproducing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```sh
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd translation && cargo build --release && cargo test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/differential.rs` will build the C reference itself (out-of-source, under
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/target/`) if `c_src/build/driver` is absent, so `cargo test` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m self-contained. Nothing in `c_src/` is written to other than the `build/`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m directory created by the documented CMake invocation; `c_src/src/main.c` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` are unmodified.
